  "notes": "The loudest end of modern country — metal-adjacent guitars and trap drums under a shouted twang. The kit is the entry's stated hybrid and no other model in this section carries it: \"programmed clap/snap on the backbeat, 16th/32nd hat rolls, sampled sub-kicks, plus a real rock kit with double-kick-adjacent 16th figures on the heaviest cuts; hard full-band stops and half-time chorus drops.\" So the kick density ceiling is the highest here, the hat rolls run at 32nds and the drop-out probability is at the top of the batch. Harmony is minor-leaning for the genre — \"i–VI–III–VII, i–VII–VI, and I–♭VII–IV on the major rockers\" — and the entry adds a voicing fact that changes `extensions` rather than the numerals: \"drop-tuned power chords rather than triads,\" which is why the third-bearing triad share is low and the seventh share carries the weight instead. The 808 is authored `counter_riff` because both layers are named: \"808-style sub plus a heavily overdriven, drop-tuned electric bass doubling the guitar riff in octaves,\" hence `distortion` at heavy and an `independent_riff` bass above it. Melody is \"shouted-sung baritone with rap-cadenced verses (high syllable density, on-the-16ths) and a chanted chorus.\" ⚠ Confidence is medium, matching the research; the per-track key/tempo readings are [LOW-CONF], and the entry notes the heaviest cuts are equally readable as snare-on-3 half-time — the folded 2&4 spine is encoded per the section convention. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the 808 register, the velocity tiers and the counter ratio.",
